Magic numbers: 'max: 20' (and max-occurrences: 21 on supported-hash-algo) hard-codes the
value range of `enum hash_algo` from `include/uapi/linux/hash_info.h` with no comment. In this
tree that enum runs to `HASH_ALGO_SHA3_512` = 22 (`HASH_ALGO__LAST` = 23), so 20 silently
excludes SHA3-256/384/512 and the choice is undocumented; 20 is also `HASH_ALGO_STREEBOG_512`,
not a 'last' marker. If hash-algo is meant to be the `hash_info.h` numbering, say so and
justify the cut-off; if not, define a CTLV enum. As is, it is an ABI constant nobody can
review. Also the bounds do not relate hash-algo to the digest length: data is 16..64
bytes for any algo, so the handler must check length == digest size for hash-algo.

